  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Line of rain showers moves through by daybreak, \nmountain showers persist through the day before slowly changing \nto snow tonight.\n\nA broken line of weakening showers in association with a cold \nfront is pushing into the Piedmont. Light rain will continue for\nthe next couple of hours before it pushes east of the area. A\nmostly sunny day is in store for the Piedmont as winds increase\nmid-morning behind the front. Sustained winds of 15 mph and \ngusts up to 30 mph are expected. \n\nThe western mountains will continue to see rain showers through\nthe day as multiple shortwaves of energy swing around a large \nupper-level trough that encompasses most of the Eastern CONUS. \nBy tonight, strong cold air advection also reaches our area, \nwhich will slowly transition rain showers to snow showers at \nthe higher elevations initially. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Upslope snow event through Saturday for the western\nmountains, much colder air through the weekend before a warmup\nby late next week. \n\nAs subsequent shortwave troughs swing through the area Thursday\nnight into Friday, upslope snow will continue across the western\nmountains of NC/VA/WV. Due to the ground being warm, initial\nsnow will mostly melt, but as the cold air settles in, light\naccumulations will occur. Flurries will be possible anywhere\nalong/west of I-81 on Friday, and generally up to 0.5\" is \npossible for the western mountains outside of the higher \nelevations, where 1-3\" is possible, especially on elevated or \ngrassy surfaces. It is possible a few areas such as Western \nGreenbrier County or Mt. Rogers could exceed 3\", but confidence \nin that occurring is low. Snow showers continue for the western \nmountains through midday Saturday before tapering off as the \nupper-trough begins to lift off to the northeast. \n\nThe cold air will be the bigger story for most, as it continues\nto pour into the area from Canada, as a large upper trough sits\noverhead. This will keep highs around 5-10 degrees below normal\nfor the Piedmont due to clear skies, while heavier cloud cover \nfrom the northwest flow will keep highs 15-20 degrees below \nnormal for locations west of the Blue Ridge. Highs each day\nthrough the weekend will be in the low 50s for the Piedmont, \nand in the 30s to low 40s for the mountains. Overnight lows \nfall into the 20s area-wide Saturday and Sunday nights. \n\nWinds will also remain elevated through the weekend, gusting to\n20-25 mph Friday and Saturday, lowering to around 20 mph Sunday\nand becoming light by Monday as high pressure builds back into \nthe area. Due to these winds along with the cold temperatures, \nwind chills will be in the teens/20s Saturday and Sunday \nmornings. While this is not a dangerous level of cold, it will \nbe quite the shock to the system after the mild last week or so.\nBy early next week, a gradual warming trend occurs, with highs \nin the 50s by Tuesday and in the 60s by Thursday with no rain \nexpected all week.",
